[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What the C program does, and every branch in it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m typedef struct { int floors; int bedrooms; double bathrooms; } house_t;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m static void print_hex(unsigned char *p, int len);   // 16 iterations, "%02x", then "\n"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m void driver(int floors);                            // house = {0}; then 3 field stores
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int main(void) { int x = 0; scanf("%d", &x); driver(x); return 0; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m There is **no** `if`, no early `return`, no length check and no null check in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the C source. `print_hex` always runs exactly `sizeof(house_t) == 16`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m iterations, `driver` is unconditional, and `main` always returns 0. Therefore
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m every behavioural branch lives in the `scanf("%d", &x)` conversion, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input classes are the states that conversion can end in. There is no error path
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that writes to stderr or returns non-zero — the C program exits 0 for all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inputs, including garbage.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Enumerated input classes (each has a test):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Class | Example input | C behaviour replicated |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | EOF before any non-whitespace | `""`, `" "`, `"\n"`, `"\t\t\t"` | `scanf` returns `EOF`, **`x` is not assigned** and keeps its initial `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Whitespace skip, incl. across newlines | `"\n\n\n42\n"`, `"\v\f\r 9"` | `%d` skips all `isspace` bytes (` \t\n\v\f\r`) and crosses newlines |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Matching failure — no digit | `"abc"`, `".5"`, `"e5"`, `",1"`, `"\0"` | `scanf` returns `0`, `x` stays `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Matching failure — sign then non-digit | `"-"`, `"+"`, `"--5"`, `"- 5"` | `scanf` returns `0`, `x` stays `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Single successful item | `"1"`, `"0"`, `"3"`, `"-1"`, `"+5"` | value assigned |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Conversion stops at first non-digit | `"5abc"`, `"12.7"`, `"0x10"`, `"5 9"`, `"1\n2"` | only the leading digit run is consumed; `0x10` is decimal `0`, not hex |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Leading zeros | `"007"`, `"0000000009"` | decimal, **not** octal (`%d` is base 10) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 32-bit extremes | `INT_MAX`, `INT_MIN` | exact |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Fits `long`, overflows `int` | `2147483648`, `4294967295`, `2^33`, `3000000000`, `-2147483649` | glibc converts with `strtol` then stores `(int) num.l` → **low 32 bits, wrapping** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Overflows `long` | `9223372036854775808`, `2^64`, 26 nines, 5000 nines | `strtol` **saturates** at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`, then truncates to `int` (`LONG_MAX` → `-1`, `LONG_MIN` → `0`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Embedded NUL | `"\0 5"`, `"5\0"` | NUL is neither space nor digit: matching failure, or terminates a digit run |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Arbitrary binary | all 256 byte values, high-bit bytes, invalid UTF-8 | matching failure; Rust must not panic on non-UTF-8 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Very long tokens / prefixes | 5000-digit runs, 9000-space prefix, 1 MB trailing input | identical; unread remainder is simply never consumed |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Translation details that had to match, and were checked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places where a plausible translation *would* have diverged. Each
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m was verified against the C binary rather than assumed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`x` is not written on a failed conversion.** `int x = 0; scanf("%d", &x);`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    leaves `x == 0` when `scanf` returns `0` or `EOF`. `scanf_i32` in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `src/main.rs` returns `Option<i32>` and `main` only assigns on `Some`, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `0` survives. A translation that used `unwrap_or(0)` would coincidentally
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    agree here, but one that errored out or exited non-zero on bad input would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    not — the C program exits **0** on `"abc"`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **Overflow is saturate-then-truncate, not wrap.** glibc's `%d` runs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `strtol` and stores `(int) num.l`. So `"9223372036854775808"` (> `LONG_MAX`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    clamps to `LONG_MAX` and prints `ffffffff` (i.e. `-1`), *not* `0` as a pure
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    128-bit wrap would give. `scanf_i32` reproduces this by accumulating in `i64`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    with `checked_*`, latching to `i64::MIN`/`i64::MAX` on overflow, then `as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    i32`. Confirmed on `LONG_MAX±1`, `LONG_MIN±1`, `2^64`, and 5000-nine inputs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **Negative values accumulate negatively.** Accumulating the magnitude and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    negating at the end would make `"-9223372036854775808"` overflow one step too
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    early. `scanf_i32` uses `checked_sub` while negative, matching `strtol`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **Struct layout and endianness.** The program hex-dumps the raw bytes of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `house_t`, so the output *is* the ABI layout: `floors` at offset 0,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `bedrooms` at offset 4, `bathrooms` (8-byte aligned) at offset 8,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `sizeof == 16`, little-endian. `house_t house = {0}` zero-fills the whole
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    object. Rust models this as a zeroed `[u8; 16]` with explicit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `to_le_bytes()` stores rather than relying on `#[repr(C)]` plus
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `transmute`, which keeps the dump deterministic. Pinned by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `a_known_good_output_shape`: input `1` ⇒
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `01000000030000000000000000000040\n` (`2.0f64` = `0x4000000000000000`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Note this layout is x86-64 System V; the tests compare against the C binary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    built on the same host, so any layout assumption is validated rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    trusted.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. **`isspace` set.** `%d` skips exactly ` `, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`, `\r` in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the C locale — `is_c_space` lists the same six. Verified per-byte by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `c_every_single_byte_input` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `c_sign_or_space_followed_by_every_byte`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6. **Output formatting.** `printf("%02x", p[i])` × 16 with no separators,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    followed by a single `printf("\n")`, and nothing else — no leading label, no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    trailing space. Rust uses `{:02x}` and one `writeln!`. Byte-compared, so a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    stray newline or uppercase hex would fail.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 7. **Write errors are ignored, exit code stays 0.** The C code never checks
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `printf`'s return value, so a failing stdout still yields `return 0`. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Rust code discards the `write!`/`flush` results for the same reason.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Checked manually with stdout redirected to `/dev/full`: both exit `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 8. **Exit status is always 0.** `main` has a single `return 0` and no path can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    abort. The Rust `main` returns `()` (status 0) and, notably, must not panic
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    on any input — non-UTF-8 stdin is read as raw bytes, never as a `String`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    which is why `c_binary_input` passes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Not covered
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Non-x86-64 / big-endian hosts: the byte image would differ from the hardcoded
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   little-endian offsets, but so would the C program's, and both are compared on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the same host, so a real divergence there is untested rather than known-good.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - A C library other than glibc: the saturate-then-truncate overflow behaviour of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `%d` is glibc's (`strtol` + `(int)` cast). Under a library that behaves
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   differently on overflow, the overflow cases would need re-checking.
